  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level trough over our region will deepen today, with a\nclosed low centered near Georgian Bay. Occluded front steadily \nprogressing eastward across our area this afternoon, which is \nproviding some forcing, along with falling heights aloft for \nshowers and some thunderstorms to continue developing.\n\nThere remains a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe storms \nacross the entire region today. CAMs and radar trends indicating\nstorm mode to be mainly cells/cluster, with perhaps a few lines\ndeveloping as shear increases later this afternoon. Breaks of \nsunshine have allowed for destabilization. SPC Mesoanalysis page\nindicating SBCAPE of 1000-1500 J/Kg and effective shear of \n30-35 kt. Environment is conducive for isolated severe storms, \nwith mainly damaging wind gusts and perhaps an instance of large\nhail if storms can grow tall enough. Convection should \ndissipate shortly after sunset this evening, as instability \nwanes and a small-scale low level ridge builds in from the west.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe core of an anomalously strong upper level low (500 mb \nheight anomalies of -2 to -3 STDEV) will gradually track east \nnear the far southern Ontario/Quebec border on Mon. A short wave\ndisturbance rotating around the periphery of the upper low core\nis expected to move across our area. This will result in \nadditional mainly scattered showers and a few T-storms Mon \nafternoon into early evening. Coverage looks to be most numerous\nacross the Adirondacks, where there will be upslope/lake-effect\nenhancement with a cold core aloft moving overhead and W-SW \nflow developing over a very warm Lake Ontario (surface \ntemperatures +20C to 22C). Due to anticipated low-topped \nconvection associated with limited instability < 700 J/Kg, the \nthreat for severe storm is low. However, there is potential for \na few storms to produce small hail and/or gusty winds due to \nsteepening lapse rates beneath the cold core aloft. \n\nThe core of the upper low will track NE across southern Quebec \non Tue, as the trailing trough axis moves east across our \nregion. This will result in additional showers developing, \nalthough there should be lower coverage compared to Mon except \nover the W. Adirondacks where lake-effect/upslope enhancement \nwill persist. It will become breezy, with westerly winds gusting\n20-25 mph at times in favored areas. Temperatures will remain \nbelow normal through Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nTranquil weather returns on Wed, as the upper level trough \nfinally shifts east and high pressure builds in across the \nregion. Unsettled conditions look to return on Thu, as the next \nupper level trough approaches from the west. Potential for \nmultiple disturbances and surface fronts make timing details \ndifficult this far out, but there is at least enough confidence \nto mention likely showers/T-storms developing Thu \nafternoon/evening. There may end up being a threat for some \nsevere storms depending on timing and sufficient \ninstability/shear. Will continue to monitor trends over the next\nfew days. The broad upper level trough still looks to be in \nplace on Fri, so there will be continued threat for \nshowers/T-storms. Coverage may not be as much as Thu if the main\ncold front pushes east of the region by Fri morning. \nTemperatures should warm back to normal levels Wed-Fri as well.",
